\DOC ENSURES_WHILE_PDOWN_TAC

\TYPE {ENSURES_WHILE_PDOWN_TAC : term -> term -> term -> term -> tactic}

\SYNOPSIS
Reduces an {ensures} goal describing a countdown loop to a single conjunctive
goal containing a nonzero-trip-count condition, a loop-entry step, two
per-iteration body/back-edge steps, and a final exit step.

\DESCRIBE
The tactic takes four terms: a trip-count term {t} giving the initial loop
counter value; two code-location terms {u1} and {u2} denoting the loop head and
the loop back-edge/test point; and a loop invariant of the form {\x1 x2. ...},
a predicate on the counter value and the machine state. Applied to an {ensures}
goal {A ?- ...}, it leaves {A} untouched and produces exactly one goal whose
conclusion is a four-part conjunction:

- {~(t = 0)}; - an {ensures} from the original precondition to the state
satisfying the invariant at {t} at the loop head {u1}; - for each iteration, a
universally quantified {ensures} over a fresh counter variable constrained by
hypotheses such as being below {t}, unequal to {t}, nonzero, and with {t}
nonzero — one going from the invariant at the successor counter at the loop
head {u1} to the invariant at the counter at the test point {u2}, the other
going back from {u2} to {u1}; - an {ensures} from the invariant instantiated at
zero at the test point {u2} to the original postcondition.

The frame (the {MAYCHANGE} component) of the original goal is reused unchanged
in every conjunct, and the program-loaded hypothesis of the original
precondition is propagated into the intermediate pre/postconditions.
{
   A ?- ensures ...
   ========================================  ENSURES_WHILE_PDOWN_TAC t u1 u2 (\x1 x2. ...)
   A ?- ~(t = 0) /\
        ensures ... /\
        (!z1. ... ==> ensures ...) /\
        (!z2. ... ==> ensures ...) /\
        ensures ...
}

\COMMENTS
The observed traces show only this single transformation of {ensures} goals for
machine-code loops, always leaving one subgoal.

\ENDDOC
